Notification provider diagnostic privacy
========================================

Scope
-----
Built-in SMTP, Gmail OAuth, Slack, Teams, generic webhook, Discord, Ntfy,
Gotify, HTTP email and Twilio providers omit raw exception text and rejected
vendor response bodies from delivery errors, verification failures and their
provider logs. These values can include credentials, recipient information,
message content or private endpoint addresses. Local input validation uses
fixed messages and never echoes a rejected email address.

Operator behavior
-----------------
Failed deliveries remain failed. HTTP status codes and fixed categories are
retained for diagnosis. HTTP 429 and 5xx remain eligible for bounded retries;
other HTTP 4xx preserve the existing permanent-failure policy. SMTP numeric
4xx replies are temporary and 5xx replies permanent. A mixed or incomplete
set of refused-recipient codes is treated conservatively as temporary.
Invalid TLS certificates and local configuration/input failures remain
visible without including certificate, address or exception details.
Malformed provider JSON is a provider failure, not a configuration refusal.

The successful provider request payloads, authentication, returned message
identities and database schema are unchanged. Use the status/category to
check provider credentials, sender authorization, rate limits or availability.
Consult the provider's authenticated administration console for additional
vendor diagnostics; do not paste credentials or message content into tickets.

Qualification
-------------
Synthetic HTTP/SMTP cases check diagnostics, verification and retry policy.
A local real STARTTLS peer checks authentication, recipient and DATA refusals;
certificate-chain/hostname rejection and acceptance/lost-ack cases remain in
the protocol suite. Before/after database qualification checks actual persisted
tracking errors and caller commit/rollback in an isolated migrated PostgreSQL.
These checks do not contact customer mail relays, vendor accounts or hardware.
The release evidence must bind them to the exact source and production images.

Limits
------
This correction does not scrub historical records, general application logs,
plugin-defined outputs, successful vendor message identifiers, template errors
or arbitrary user-supplied content. Historical diagnostic review follows the
operator's retention and incident-response policy; no records are deleted by
this change. No secret exposure or credential rotation is presumed.

Generic notification dispatch still lacks a transactional outbox. A process
failure after provider acceptance and before the database commit can cause
uncertain or duplicate delivery. Cancellation cannot recall a message already
accepted by a provider. No exactly-once or enterprise-availability guarantee is
created by sanitizing diagnostics.
